<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jablunkov Pass</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jablunkov Pass is a mountain pass in the Western Beskids on the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, at about 550 metres. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it has carried a key route from Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a>, now Slovakia, and it was fortified against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and Hungarian rebels in the 16th and 17th centuries. A German raid there on the eve of the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1939 is known as the Jablunkov incident.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jablunkov-pass.jpg" alt="Jablunkov Pass" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J.M. Czarnecki · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jablunkov_Pass_from_the_southern_slopes_of_the_Girova,_looking_NW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
